Consider the following C like code that involves pointers and dynamic memory allocation.
int* allocateAndFillArray(int size) {
   int* arr = (int*) malloc(size * sizeof(int));
   for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
      arr[i] = i * 2;
   }
   return arr;
}

int main() {
   int n = 5;
   int* p = allocateAndFillArray(n);
   int sum = 0;
   for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
      sum += p[i];
   }
   return sum;
}

Generate the three-address code(TAC) for the above code. Assume that the TAC has an instruction alloc(n) that performs the malloc().

Consider the following C program, that transposes a 3 x 3 matrix and sums the diagonal elements:

for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
   for (int j = 0; j < 3; j++) {
      transpose[j][i] = arr[i][j];
      if (i == j) {
         sum = sum + transpose[i][j];
      }
   }
}

Convert the above code into three-address code and partition the same into basic blocks. Construct the flow graph for the basic blocks, showing control flow between them.

a. Consider the following C code, that uses a switch-case statement to compute different operations based on a input value.

int result;
int x = 5;
switch(x) {
   case 1:
   result = x + 1;
   break;
   case 2:
   result = x * 2;
   break;
   case 3:
   result = x - 3;
   break;
   default:
   result = x;
}

Write semantic actions to generate intermediate code for the above switch-case statement. Show the annotated parse tree for the switch case statement.

Explain the structure of a compiler. Illustrate the output of each phase of compilation for the given input:

